Article 14 According to the provisions of the Criminal Procedure Law, except for the following circumstances, criminal cases shall be under the jurisdiction of public security organs:
(1) Duty-related criminal cases under the jurisdiction of supervisory authorities;
(2) Crimes such as illegal detention, extortion of confessions through torture, and illegal search committed by judicial officers by taking advantage of their functions and powers, which are discovered in the course of legal supervision over litigation activities and are under the jurisdiction of the People's Procuratorate, as well as major criminal cases committed by state functionaries of organs under the jurisdiction of public security organs by taking advantage of their functions and powers, which are decided to be placed on file for investigation by the People's Procuratorate at or above the provincial level;
(3) Cases of private prosecution under the jurisdiction of the People's Court. For minor criminal cases for which the victim has evidence and which are directly accepted by the People's Court, if the People's Court dismisses the prosecution due to insufficient evidence and transfers the case to the public security organ, or if the victim files a complaint with the public security organ, the public security organ shall accept the case; if the victim directly files a complaint with the public security organ, the public security organ shall accept it;
(4) Crimes of dereliction of duty by servicemen and criminal cases occurring within the military under the jurisdiction of the military security departments;
(5) Criminal cases of crimes committed by prisoners within a prison under the jurisdiction of the prison;
(6) Criminal cases occurring in the sea areas under China's jurisdiction beyond the sea (island) coastline under the jurisdiction of the coast guard departments. For cases occurring in areas such as coastal harbors and inlets, docks, tidal flats, and berthing points for Taiwan fishing boats, they shall be under the jurisdiction of public security organs;
(7) Other criminal cases that shall be under the jurisdiction of other organs according to law and regulations.

Article 21 Where several public security organs have jurisdiction over a criminal case, the public security organ that first accepted the case shall have jurisdiction. When necessary, the public security organ in the place of the principal crime may have jurisdiction.
Under any of the following circumstances, the public security organ may, within its scope of duties, conduct combined investigation:
(1) One person commits several crimes;
(2) Joint crimes;
(3) A criminal suspect of a joint crime also commits other crimes; or
(4) The crimes committed by multiple criminal suspects are related, and combined handling is conducive to ascertaining the facts of the crimes.

Article 24 The public security organ at the county level shall be responsible for investigating criminal cases occurring within its jurisdiction.
The public security organ at or above the city level with districts shall be responsible for investigating major cases of the following crimes:
(1) Crimes endangering national security;
(2) Terrorist activity crimes;
(3) Crimes involving foreign affairs;
(4) Economic crimes;
(5) Group crimes;
(6) Cross-regional crimes.
Where a superior public security organ deems it necessary, it may investigate a criminal case under the jurisdiction of a subordinate public security organ; where a subordinate public security organ considers that a case is major and requires investigation by a superior public security organ, it may request the public security organ at the next higher level to have jurisdiction.

Article 32 The responsible person of a public security organ or an investigator shall, under any of the following circumstances, voluntarily file an application for withdrawal; if no such application is filed voluntarily, he or she shall be ordered to withdraw, and the party concerned and his or her statutory agent shall also have the right to request their withdrawal:
(1) Being a party to the case or a close relative of a party;
(2) Having an interest in the case personally or through a close relative;
(3) Having served as a witness, expert evaluator, defender, or litigation agent in the case; or
(4) Having any other relationship with a party to the case that may affect the impartial handling of the case.

Article 33 The responsible person of a public security organ or an investigator shall not engage in any of the following acts:
(1) Meeting with a party to the case or his or her authorized representative in violation of regulations;
(2) Demanding or accepting property or other benefits from a party to the case or his or her authorized representative;
(3) Accepting entertainment or participating in activities paid for by a party to the case or his or her authorized representative; or
(4) Other improper acts that may affect the impartial handling of the case.
Whoever violates the preceding paragraph shall be ordered to withdraw and shall be subject to legal liability according to law. The party concerned and his or her statutory agent shall have the right to request his or her withdrawal.

Article 42 The public security organ shall ensure that defense lawyers engage in the following professional activities according to law during the investigation stage:
(1) Learning from the public security organ about the crimes suspected of by the criminal suspect and the relevant circumstances of the case, and presenting opinions;
(2) Meeting and communicating with the criminal suspect, and learning from the criminal suspect about the relevant circumstances of the case;
(3) Providing legal assistance to the criminal suspect, and acting on his/her behalf in petitions and accusations;
(4) Applying for modification of compulsory measures for the criminal suspect.

Article 46 Where a criminal suspect falls under any of the following circumstances and has not retained a defender, the public security organ shall, within three days from the date of discovering such circumstance, notify a legal aid agency to assign a defense lawyer to the criminal suspect:
(1) The criminal suspect is blind, deaf, or mute, or is a mentally ill person who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his/her own conduct;
(2) The criminal suspect may be sentenced to life imprisonment or the death penalty.

Article 52 For cases involving crimes endangering national security or terrorist activities, the case-handling department shall, when sending the criminal suspect to the detention house for custody, notify the detention house in writing; if the criminal suspect is under residential surveillance, it shall notify the enforcement authority in writing when handing him/her over for enforcement.
Where a defense lawyer requests during the investigation stage to meet with a criminal suspect in custody or under residential surveillance in a case specified in the preceding paragraph, he/she shall apply to the case-handling department.
With respect to the meeting application filed by the defense lawyer, the case-handling department shall, within three days after receiving the application, submit it to the person in charge of the public security organ at or above the county level for approval, make a decision to permit or not to permit, notify the defense lawyer in writing, and promptly notify the detention house or the department executing residential surveillance. Except where it would hinder the investigation or might divulge state secrets, the decision to permit the meeting shall be made.
If the public security organ does not permit the meeting, it shall state the reasons. After the circumstances that hindered the investigation or might have divulged state secrets disappear, the public security organ shall permit the meeting.
The circumstances that "hinder the investigation" as mentioned in this Article include:
(1) Where it might destroy or falsify evidence, interfere with a witness's testimony, or lead to collusion;
(2) Where it might cause the criminal suspect to cause self-harm, commit suicide, or escape;
(3) Where it might cause other offenders in the same case to evade or obstruct the investigation;
(4) Where the family members of the criminal suspect are implicated in the crime.

Article 59 All materials that can be used to prove the facts of a case are evidence.
Evidence includes:
(1) Physical evidence;
(2) Documentary evidence;
(3) Testimony of witnesses;
(4) Statements of victims;
(5) Confessions and exculpations of criminal suspects;
(6) Expert evaluation opinions;
(7) Records of inspection, examination, investigative experimentation, search, sealing, seizure, extraction, identification, etc.;
(8) Audio-visual materials and electronic data.
Evidence must be verified before it can be used as the basis for determining the facts of a case.

Article 69 The case facts that need to be ascertained include:
(1) Whether a criminal act exists;
(2) The time, place, method, consequences, and other circumstances of the criminal act;
(3) Whether the criminal act was committed by a criminal suspect;
(4) The identity of the criminal suspect;
(5) The motive and purpose of the criminal suspect in committing the criminal act;
(6) The criminal suspect's liability and relationship with other accomplices;
(7) Whether the criminal suspect has any statutory circumstances for heavier, lighter, or mitigated punishment, or exemption from punishment;
(8) Other facts relevant to the case.

Article 75 For cases involving crimes endangering national security, terrorist activities, organized crimes of a mafia nature, drug crimes, etc., if a witness, expert evaluator, or victim, due to testifying during the investigation process, faces danger to the personal safety of themselves or their close relatives, the public security organ shall take one or more of the following protective measures:
(1) Not disclosing personal information such as true names, addresses, communication methods, and work units;
(2) Prohibiting specific persons from contacting the protected person;
(3) Taking specialized protective measures for the protected person's person and residence;
(4) Moving the protected person to a safe place for protection;
(5) Changing the protected person's residence and name;
(6) Other necessary protective measures.
If a witness, expert evaluator, or victim considers that, due to testifying during the investigation process, they or their close relatives face danger to personal safety, and requests protection from the public security organ, and the public security organ, upon review, considers that the conditions in the preceding paragraph are met and that protective measures are indeed necessary, it shall take one or more of the above protective measures.
A public security organ may require relevant entities or individuals to cooperate when taking protective measures according to law.
When a case is transferred for review and prosecution, the relevant information regarding the protective measures taken shall be transferred to the People's Procuratorate together.

Article 81 A public security organ may grant release on bail pending investigation or trial to a criminal suspect in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The suspect may be sentenced to public surveillance, criminal detention, or independently subject to a supplementary punishment;
(2) The suspect may be sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment or a heavier punishment, and the grant of release on bail pending investigation or trial will not create any danger to society;
(3) The suspect is a woman who suffers from a serious disease, is unable to take care of herself, is pregnant, or is nursing her own infant, and the grant of release on bail pending investigation or trial will not create any danger to society;
(4) The period of custody has expired, the case has not yet been concluded, and further investigation is needed.
For a criminal suspect who has been detained, where the evidence does not meet the conditions for arrest, or after arrest is requested, the People's Procuratorate does not approve the arrest, and further investigation is needed, if the conditions for release on bail pending investigation or trial are met, release on bail pending investigation or trial may be granted according to law.

Article 85 Where a guarantor provides a guarantee, the guarantor must meet the following conditions and be examined and approved by the public security organ:
(1) Having no involvement in the case;
(2) Having the ability to perform the guarantee obligations;
(3) Enjoying political rights and not being subject to restrictions on personal freedom;
(4) Having a fixed domicile and income.

Article 89 When announcing the decision on release on bail pending investigation or trial, a public security organ shall inform the person released on bail to observe the following provisions:
(1) Not to leave the city or county where he or she resides without the approval of the executing authority;
(2) To report to the executing authority within twenty-four hours if his or her address, work unit, or contact information changes;
(3) To appear before the authority in a timely manner when summoned;
(4) Not to interfere with witnesses giving testimony in any form;
(5) Not to destroy or falsify evidence or collude with others in making confessions.

Article 90 When deciding to grant release on bail pending investigation or trial, a public security organ may also, based on the circumstances of the case, order the person released on bail to observe one or more of the following provisions:
(1) Not to enter specific places related to his or her criminal activities;
(2) Not to meet or communicate by any means with witnesses, victims and their close relatives, accomplices, or other specific persons related to the case;
(3) Not to engage in specific activities related to his or her criminal acts;
(4) To surrender his or her passport and other exit-entry documents, and driver's license to the executing authority for safekeeping.
A public security organ shall determine the scope of specific places, specific persons, and specific activities by comprehensively considering factors such as the nature and circumstances of the case, social impact, and the criminal suspect's social connections.

Article 93 The police station executing the release on bail pending investigation or trial shall perform the following duties:
(1) Inform the person released on bail of the provisions that must be observed, and the legal consequences of violating such provisions or committing new crimes during the period of release on bail;
(2) Supervise and inspect the person released on bail to observe relevant provisions, and promptly keep track of the person's activities, address, work unit, contact information, and any changes;
(3) Supervise the guarantor to perform the guarantee obligations;
(4) If the person released on bail violates the provisions that shall be observed or the guarantor fails to perform the guarantee obligations, promptly stop the violation, take emergency measures, and at the same time inform the decision-making authority.

Article 109 The public security organ may place under residential surveillance a criminal suspect who meets the conditions for arrest and falls under any of the following circumstances:
(1) suffering from a serious illness and unable to take care of himself;
(2) being a woman who is pregnant or is nursing her own infant;
(3) being the sole supporter of a person who cannot take care of himself;
(4) due to the special circumstances of the case or the needs of handling the case, it is more appropriate to adopt residential surveillance;
(5) the period of custody has expired, the case has not yet been concluded, and it is necessary to adopt residential surveillance.
Where a People's Procuratorate decides not to approve arrest, and the criminal suspect needs further investigation and meets the conditions for residential surveillance, he may be placed under residential surveillance.
Where the conditions for release on bail pending investigation or trial are met, but the criminal suspect cannot propose a guarantor or pay a bond, he may be placed under residential surveillance.
Where the person released on bail pending investigation or trial violates the provisions of Articles 89 and 90 of these Provisions, he may be placed under residential surveillance.

Article 111 Residential surveillance shall be executed at the domicile of the criminal suspect or defendant; if there is no fixed domicile, it may be executed at a designated residence. For crimes suspected of endangering national security or terrorist activities, if execution at the domicile may hinder investigation, with the approval of the public security organ at the next higher level, it may also be executed at a designated residence.
Any of the following circumstances falls under the term 'may hinder investigation' as provided in this Article:
(1) it may result in destruction or falsification of evidence, interference with witnesses giving testimony, or collusion in making confessions;
(2) it may cause the criminal suspect to injure himself, commit suicide, or escape;
(3) it may cause other accomplices to evade or obstruct investigation;
(4) the criminal suspect or defendant may be in personal danger if residential surveillance is executed at the domicile;
(5) the family members or personnel of the work unit of the criminal suspect or defendant are involved in the crime.
Where residential surveillance is executed at a designated residence, the person under residential surveillance shall not be required to pay fees.

Article 112 A fixed domicile means the lawful residence where the person under residential surveillance lives within the city or county where the case-handling authority is located; a designated residence means the living residence designated by the public security organ for the person under residential surveillance within the city or county where the case-handling authority is located based on the circumstances of the case.
The designated residence shall meet the following conditions:
(1) having normal living and rest conditions;
(2) being convenient for surveillance and management;
(3) ensuring safety.
The public security organ shall not execute residential surveillance in a custodial facility, a special case-handling site, or an office premises.

Article 113 Where residential surveillance is executed at a designated residence, except where it is impossible to notify, a written notice of residential surveillance shall be prepared and, within 24 hours after the execution of residential surveillance, the authority that made the decision shall notify the family members of the person under residential surveillance.
Any of the following circumstances falls under the term 'impossible to notify' as provided in this Article:
(1) the person does not provide his true name, address, and identity is unknown;
(2) there are no family members;
(3) the contact information provided for family members cannot be reached;
(4) notification is impossible due to force majeure such as natural disasters.
After the circumstances making notification impossible disappear, the family members of the person under residential surveillance shall be notified immediately.
If it is impossible to notify the family members, the reason shall be noted in the written notice of residential surveillance.

Article 115 When announcing the decision on residential surveillance, the public security organ shall inform the person under residential surveillance that he must comply with the following provisions:
(1) he shall not leave the premises where residential surveillance is executed without the approval of the executing authority;
(2) he shall not meet with others or communicate by any means without the approval of the executing authority;
(3) he shall appear before the authority in a timely manner when summoned;
(4) he shall not interfere with witnesses giving testimony in any form;
(5) he shall not destroy or falsify evidence or collude in confessions;
(6) he shall deliver his passport and other exit-entry documents, identity card, and driving license to the executing authority for keeping.

Article 124 A public security organ may detain in advance a flagrant offender or a major suspect under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The person is preparing to commit a crime, is committing a crime, or is discovered immediately after committing a crime;
(2) The victim or an eyewitness identifies the person as the offender;
(3) Evidence of a crime is found on the person or at the person's residence;
(4) The person attempts to commit suicide, escape, or is at large after committing a crime;
(5) The person is likely to destroy or falsify evidence or collude with others;
(6) The person does not provide true name or address, and identity is unknown;
(7) The person is under strong suspicion of committing crimes across different regions, committing multiple crimes, or committing crimes in a group.

Article 131 After reviewing a detained criminal suspect, based on the circumstances of the case and upon approval by the head of the public security organ at or above the county level, the following actions shall be taken respectively:
(1) If arrest is necessary, the formalities for submitting an application for approval of arrest shall be handled according to law within the detention period;
(2) If criminal liability shall be investigated but arrest is not necessary, the case shall be directly transferred to the People's Procuratorate for examination and prosecution according to law, or after handling the formalities for release on bail pending investigation or residential surveillance according to law, the case shall be transferred to the People's Procuratorate for examination and prosecution;
(3) If the detention period expires but the case is not yet completed and further investigation is necessary, the formalities for release on bail pending investigation or residential surveillance shall be handled according to law;
(4) If any of the circumstances provided in Article 186 of these Provisions exists, the detained person shall be released and a release certificate shall be issued; if administrative handling is necessary, the person shall be handled according to law or transferred to the relevant department.

Article 133 For a criminal suspect for whom there is evidence to support the facts of a crime and who may be sentenced to a punishment of imprisonment or above, if the imposition of release on bail pending investigation is not sufficient to prevent the following social dangers, approval of arrest shall be sought:
(1) The suspect may commit a new crime;
(2) There is a real danger of endangering national security, public security, or social order;
(3) The suspect may destroy or falsify evidence, interfere with witness testimony, or collude with others;
(4) The suspect may retaliate against the victim, informant, or accuser;
(5) The suspect attempts to commit suicide or escape.
For a criminal suspect for whom there is evidence to support the facts of a crime and who may be sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment of 10 years or more, or for whom there is evidence to support the facts of a crime, who may be sentenced to a punishment of imprisonment or above, and who has previously intentionally committed a crime or whose identity is unknown, approval of arrest shall be sought.
When the public security organ submits an application to the People's Procuratorate for examination and approval of arrest in accordance with the provisions of paragraph 1, it shall state the reasons why the criminal suspect presents social danger.

Article 134 'There is evidence to support the facts of a crime' means that the following circumstances are simultaneously present:
(1) There is evidence to prove that the facts of a crime have occurred;
(2) There is evidence to prove that the facts of the crime were committed by the criminal suspect;
(3) The evidence proving that the criminal suspect committed the criminal act has been verified as true.
The 'facts of a crime' provided in the preceding paragraph may be the facts of a single criminal act or the facts of any one of several criminal acts.

Article 135 Where a person released on bail pending investigation violates the provisions on release on bail pending investigation and falls under any of the following circumstances, approval of arrest may be sought:
(1) The person is suspected of intentionally committing a new criminal act;
(2) There is a real danger of endangering national security, public security, or social order;
(3) The person has committed acts of destroying or falsifying evidence or interfering with witness testimony or colluding with others, which are sufficient to affect the normal progress of the investigation;
(4) The person has retaliated against the victim, informant, or accuser;
(5) The person attempts to commit suicide or escape to evade investigation;
(6) The person, without approval, leaves the city or county where the person resides, with serious circumstances, or leaves without approval on two or more occasions;
(7) The person, upon summons, fails to appear without justifiable reasons, with serious circumstances, or fails to appear after two or more summons;
(8) The person violates provisions by entering a specific place, engaging in a specific activity, or meeting or corresponding with a specific person on two or more occasions.

Article 136 Where a person under residential surveillance violates the provisions on residential surveillance and falls under any of the following circumstances, approval of arrest may be sought:
(1) The person is suspected of intentionally committing a new criminal act;
(2) The person has committed acts of destroying or falsifying evidence or interfering with witness testimony or colluding with others, which are sufficient to affect the normal progress of the investigation;
(3) The person has retaliated against the victim, informant, or accuser;
(4) The person attempts to commit suicide or escape to evade investigation;
(5) The person, without approval, leaves the place where residential surveillance is being executed, with serious circumstances, or leaves without approval on two or more occasions;
(6) The person, without approval, meets others or corresponds, with serious circumstances, or meets others or corresponds without approval on two or more occasions;
(7) The person, upon summons, fails to appear without justifiable reasons, with serious circumstances, or fails to appear after two or more summons.

Article 149 For the following types of cases that cannot be concluded upon expiration of the period specified in Article 148 of these Provisions, a written opinion requesting approval for extension of the investigative custody period shall be prepared, submitted to the responsible person of the public security organ at or above the county level for approval, and then, seven days before the expiration of the period, sent to the same-level People's Procuratorate for transmission level by level to the People's Procuratorate of the province, autonomous region, or municipality directly under the Central Government for approval of an extension of two months:
(1) Major and complex cases in remote areas with extremely inconvenient transportation;
(2) Major criminal group cases;
(3) Major and complex cases involving itinerant criminal activities;
(4) Major and complex cases involving a wide range of crimes and difficulty in obtaining evidence.

After investigation, if any of the following circumstances is found, the case shall be dismissed:
(1) no crime has occurred;
(2) the circumstances are obviously minor and the harm is insignificant, so it is not deemed a crime;
(3) the statute of limitations for prosecution has expired;
(4) the punishment has been exempted by a special amnesty decree;
(5) the criminal suspect has died;
(6) other circumstances under which criminal liability shall not be investigated according to law.
If, after investigation, it is found that a crime has occurred and criminal liability shall be investigated, but the crime was not committed by the criminal suspect under investigation, or in a joint crime, some criminal suspects are not subject to criminal punishment, the investigation of the relevant criminal suspect(s) shall be terminated, and the investigation of the case shall continue.

When carrying out investigation activities such as inspection, examination, search, identification, sealing-up, and seizure, the public security organ shall invite relevant citizens to serve as witnesses.
The following persons shall not serve as witnesses for investigation activities:
(1) persons who are physically or mentally disabled or are minors, and lack the ability to discern or cannot correctly express themselves;
(2) persons who have an interest in the case and might affect the impartial handling of the case;
(3) staff members of the public security organ or persons employed by it.
If, due to objective reasons, it is truly impossible to have a qualified person serve as a witness, the entire investigation activity shall be recorded by audio and video, and the relevant circumstances shall be noted in the record.

If a party, defender, litigation agent, or interested person has any of the following conduct by the public security organ or its investigators, they have the right to file a complaint or accusation with that organ:
(1) failing to release, lift, or modify compulsory measures after the statutory time limit has expired;
(2) failing to return the deposit for release on bail pending investigation or trial that should be returned;
(3) taking measures to seal up, seize, or freeze property unrelated to the case;
(4) failing to lift sealing-up, seizure, or freezing that should be lifted;
(5) embezzling, misappropriating, privately dividing, swapping, or using in violation of regulations the sealed-up, seized, or frozen property.
The public security organ that receives the complaint or accusation shall promptly conduct an investigation and verification, and make a handling decision within thirty days from the date of receiving the complaint or accusation, and reply to the complainant or accuser in writing. If it finds that the public security organ or its investigators have any of the above conduct, it shall immediately correct it.

Article 224: When executing a detention or arrest, in the event of any of the following emergency circumstances, a search may be conducted without a search warrant:
(1) The person may be carrying lethal weapons;
(2) The person may be concealing dangerous articles such as explosives or highly toxic substances;
(3) The person may be concealing, destroying, or transferring criminal evidence;
(4) The person may be harboring other criminal suspects;
(5) Other sudden emergency circumstances.

Article 254 Upon review, if any of the following circumstances is found, upon approval by the responsible person of the public security organ at or above the county level, a supplementary expert evaluation shall be conducted:
(1) There is an obvious omission in the evaluation content;
(2) New evidentiary material with evaluation significance is discovered;
(3) There is a new evaluation requirement for the evaluated evidentiary material;
(4) The expert opinion is incomplete and the entrusted matters cannot be determined;
(5) Other circumstances requiring a supplementary evaluation.
Upon review, if the circumstances do not meet the above conditions, upon approval by the responsible person of the public security organ at or above the county level, a decision not to permit supplementary evaluation shall be made, and the applicant shall be notified in writing within three days after the decision is made.

Article 255 Upon review, if any of the following circumstances is found, upon approval by the responsible person of the public security organ at or above the county level, a new expert evaluation shall be conducted:
(1) The evaluation procedure is illegal or violates relevant professional technical requirements;
(2) The evaluation institution or expert evaluator does not have the qualifications and conditions for evaluation;
(3) The expert evaluator intentionally makes a false evaluation or violates the recusal provisions;
(4) The basis for the expert opinion is obviously insufficient;
(5) The inspection material is false or damaged;
(6) Other circumstances requiring a new evaluation.
For a new evaluation, a different expert evaluator shall be designated or appointed.
Upon review, if the circumstances do not meet the above conditions, upon approval by the responsible person of the public security organ at or above the county level, a decision not to permit a new evaluation shall be made, and the applicant shall be notified in writing within three days after the decision is made.

Article 263: After placing a case on file, a public security organ may, based on the needs of criminal investigation, adopt technical investigation measures for the following criminal cases that seriously endanger society:
(1) Crimes endangering national security, terrorist activities, organized crimes of a gangland nature, and major drug crimes;
(2) Serious violent crimes such as intentional homicide, intentional injury causing serious injury or death, rape, robbery, kidnapping, arson, explosion, and release of dangerous substances;
(3) Major crimes of a group, serial, or cross-regional nature;
(4) Major crimes committed through telecommunications, computer networks, postal or courier channels, and major crimes committed against computer networks;
(5) Other crimes that seriously endanger society and are punishable by law with a fixed-term imprisonment of seven years or more.
When pursuing and capturing a criminal suspect or defendant who is wanted or has been approved or decided for arrest and is at large, a public security organ may adopt technical investigation measures necessary for the pursuit.

Article 283: A case concluded after investigation shall meet all of the following conditions:
(1) the facts of the case are clear;
(2) the evidence is reliable and sufficient;
(3) the nature of the crime and the charge are correctly determined;
(4) the legal formalities are complete; and
(5) criminal liability shall be investigated according to law.

Article 285: For a case concluded after investigation, the investigator shall prepare a case closure report.
The case closure report shall include the following:
(1) the basic information of the criminal suspect;
(2) whether compulsory measures have been taken and the reasons therefor;
(3) the facts and evidence of the case; and
(4) the legal basis and proposed handling.

Article 296: For a case returned by the People's Procuratorate for supplementary investigation, depending on the different circumstances, after reporting to and obtaining approval from the person responsible for a public security organ at or above the county level, the following handling shall be made respectively:
(1) where the originally determined criminal facts are unclear or the evidence is insufficient, after clarifying the facts and supplementing the evidence, a supplementary investigation report shall be prepared and transferred to the People's Procuratorate for examination; for matters that truly cannot be ascertained or evidence that cannot be supplemented, the circumstances shall be explained in writing to the People's Procuratorate;
(2) where, during the supplementary investigation, a new accomplice or a new crime is discovered, and criminal liability needs to be investigated, a new written opinion on prosecution shall be prepared and transferred to the People's Procuratorate for examination;
(3) where it is discovered that the originally determined criminal facts have undergone major changes and criminal liability should not be investigated, the case shall be dismissed or the investigation of the criminal suspect shall be terminated, and the relevant circumstances shall be notified to the People's Procuratorate that returned the case for supplementary investigation;
(4) where the originally determined criminal facts are clear and the evidence is reliable and sufficient, and the People's Procuratorate's return for supplementary investigation is inappropriate, the reasons shall be explained and the case shall be transferred to the People's Procuratorate for examination.

Article 310 For a criminal under temporary service of sentence outside prison, if any of the following circumstances exists, the public security organ that approved the temporary service of sentence outside prison shall make a decision to have the criminal taken back into prison for enforcement:
(1) it is discovered that the conditions for temporary service of sentence outside prison are not met;
(2) seriously violating the regulatory provisions concerning supervision and management of temporary service of sentence outside prison;
(3) after the circumstances for temporary service of sentence outside prison have ceased to exist, the criminal's sentence has not yet expired.
If a decision is made to have a criminal under temporary service of sentence outside prison taken back into prison for enforcement, the detention house at the place of temporary service of sentence outside prison shall carry out the enforcement.
If a criminal who does not meet the conditions for temporary service of sentence outside prison is granted such service through illegal means such as bribery, or if the criminal escapes during the period of temporary service of sentence outside prison, after the criminal is taken back into prison for enforcement, the detention house where the criminal is held shall propose that the period not be counted toward the execution of the sentence, and after examination and approval by the public security organ at or above the level of a city with districts, the matter shall be submitted to the intermediate People's Court or above at the place where the prison is located for examination and ruling.

Article 312 A criminal deprived of political rights shall observe the following provisions during the enforcement period:
(1) abide by national laws, administrative regulations, and relevant provisions formulated by the Ministry of Public Security, and submit to supervision and management;
(2) shall not enjoy the right to vote and the right to be elected;
(3) shall not organize or participate in assemblies, processions, demonstrations, or association activities;
(4) shall not publish, produce, or distribute books or audio-visual products;
(5) shall not accept interviews or deliver speeches;
(6) shall not make statements, either domestically or abroad, that damage national honor or interests, or that are otherwise socially harmful;
(7) shall not hold any position in state organs;
(8) shall not hold any leading position in state-owned companies, enterprises, institutions, or people's organizations.

Cases with any of the following circumstances shall not be considered as criminal cases arising from civil disputes:
(1) hiring someone to harm another person;
(2) involving organized crime of a mafia-like nature;
(3) involving picking quarrels and causing trouble;
(4) involving gathering people to engage in affrays;
(5) intentionally harming another person's body multiple times;
(6) other circumstances not suitable for reconciliation.

The reconciliation agreement shall include the following contents:
(1) the basic facts of the case and the main evidence;
(2) the criminal suspect admits to the crime committed, has no objection to the alleged criminal facts, and sincerely repents;
(3) the criminal suspect obtains the victim's understanding by apologizing, compensating for losses, or other means; if compensation for losses is involved, the amount and method of compensation shall be specified; if a collateral civil action is initiated, the plaintiff of the collateral civil action shall withdraw the collateral civil action;
(4) the victim voluntarily reconciles and requests or agrees to a lenient punishment for the criminal suspect according to law.
The reconciliation agreement shall be performed promptly.

Article 340 The written opinion on confiscation of illegal gains shall include the following contents:
(a) Basic information of the criminal suspect;
(b) Facts of the crime and relevant evidence materials;
(c) The circumstances of the criminal suspect's absconding, being wanted, or death;
(d) The types, quantities, and locations of the illegal gains and other property involved of the criminal suspect;
(e) The circumstances of sealing up, seizure, inquiry, and freezing, etc.
